WEBVTT

NOTE English translation of the Vietnamese narration script. Timing is distributed proportionally within each slide segment, since the narration itself is in Vietnamese.

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:04.292
Hello everyone, this is the revision video for Chapter Three:

2
00:00:04.292 --> 00:00:06.238
Communication in specific settings.

3
00:00:06.238 --> 00:00:09.100
The same skill, but every context has its own rules.

4
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.862
Within an organisation, when receiving a task from a superior: listen, take notes,

5
00:00:15.862 --> 00:00:19.374
ask questions to clarify the requirements, deadline and resources,

6
00:00:19.374 --> 00:00:23.577
then confirm back by message or email so both sides understand it the same way.

7
00:00:23.577 --> 00:00:25.764
When reporting: be proactive and on time,

8
00:00:25.764 --> 00:00:29.019
state the result first and explain afterwards; in particular,

9
00:00:29.019 --> 00:00:32.700
report bad news early together with a plan to fix it — never hide it.

10
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.771
With subordinates, assigning work must state the goal,

11
00:00:37.771 --> 00:00:39.650
the deadline and the standard clearly.

12
00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:43.409
The golden rule for evaluation: praise in public, promptly and specifically;

13
00:00:43.409 --> 00:00:46.822
criticise in private, aimed at the behaviour and never at the person,

14
00:00:46.822 --> 00:00:48.800
and always paired with a way to improve.

15
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.060
This is the most important content in the chapter:

16
00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:56.919
the LAST process for handling customer complaints.

17
00:00:56.919 --> 00:01:01.506
L is for Listen — hear them out fully, without interrupting or getting defensive.

18
00:01:01.506 --> 00:01:05.107
A is for Apologize — a sincere apology for the poor experience,

19
00:01:05.107 --> 00:01:07.451
even before it's clear whose fault it is.

20
00:01:07.451 --> 00:01:11.132
S is for Solve — offer a concrete solution with a clear deadline.

21
00:01:11.132 --> 00:01:16.568
And T is for Thank — thank the customer for the feedback and follow through until it's resolved.

22
00:01:16.568 --> 00:01:22.400
A customer whose complaint is handled well is often more loyal than one who never had a problem at all.

23
00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:27.254
At the banquet table, remember: arrive on time,

24
00:01:27.254 --> 00:01:31.587
wait for the host to invite you and assign seats, since seating reflects hierarchy;

25
00:01:31.587 --> 00:01:34.093
use utensils from the outside in; when toasting,

26
00:01:34.093 --> 00:01:36.756
the person of lower status holds their glass lower;

27
00:01:36.756 --> 00:01:40.149
respect anyone who doesn't drink alcohol and never pressure them.

28
00:01:40.149 --> 00:01:43.843
Keep conversation light: food, sport, travel; avoid politics, religion,

29
00:01:43.843 --> 00:01:45.300
income and personal matters.

30
00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.641
In a cross-cultural setting, you need to recognise four differences:

31
00:01:51.641 --> 00:01:56.452
direct versus indirect speech; how much hierarchy is valued; attitudes toward time;

32
00:01:56.452 --> 00:01:58.365
and specific gestures and taboos.

33
00:01:58.365 --> 00:01:59.059
For example,

34
00:01:59.059 --> 00:02:04.500
the phrase “we will consider it” in Japanese culture is very likely a polite way of saying no.

35
00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:08.287
Three things to remember.

36
00:02:08.287 --> 00:02:11.726
A strong internal culture makes for strong external relations.

37
00:02:11.726 --> 00:02:15.166
A customer complaint is an opportunity — use the LAST process.

38
00:02:15.166 --> 00:02:18.918
And in cross-cultural situations: learn beforehand, observe, adapt,

39
00:02:18.918 --> 00:02:21.046
and never judge by your own standards.

40
00:02:21.046 --> 00:02:24.929
Go ahead and do the forty multiple-choice questions for Chapter Three.
